1
00:00:00,389 --> 00:00:03,539
Put yourself at the moment before a fix
is handed off.

2
00:00:03,614 --> 00:00:05,939
How do you know it solves the right
problem?

3
00:00:05,989 --> 00:00:09,536
We will use a fixed regression test and
an independent human review.

4
00:00:10,164 --> 00:00:13,864
In this order service, the agent can
choose how to inspect and edit the

5
00:00:13,914 --> 00:00:16,514
validator inside the planned scope.

6
00:00:16,589 --> 00:00:19,858
Each transition in the workflow depends
on a visible result.

7
00:00:21,376 --> 00:00:25,251
Read the issue and write down the
expected answer for each example.

8
00:00:25,326 --> 00:00:27,226
One and five should be accepted.

9
00:00:27,326 --> 00:00:29,513
Zero and negative one should be
rejected.

10
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:34,225
Use the zero case for the regression
test, and keep the earlier cases in the

11
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:35,250
suite.

12
00:00:35,325 --> 00:00:38,775
These fixed inputs give us something
concrete to check before and after the

13
00:00:38,825 --> 00:00:39,226
edit.

14
00:00:40,751 --> 00:00:44,126
Run the suite with the regression in
place and the original code still

15
00:00:44,176 --> 00:00:45,201
present.

16
00:00:45,276 --> 00:00:46,751
Pause at the failure.

17
00:00:46,826 --> 00:00:50,901
It's the zero-case assertion, and the
validator returned true even though we

18
00:00:50,951 --> 00:00:51,960
expected false.

19
00:00:52,572 --> 00:00:55,072
An import error would tell a different
story.

20
00:00:55,147 --> 00:00:57,472
Capture the assertion and its revision.

21
00:00:57,547 --> 00:01:01,033
Now we have evidence that the new test
actually detects the defect.

22
00:01:02,493 --> 00:01:05,243
Before asking for the edit, agree on a
gate.

23
00:01:05,293 --> 00:01:09,018
The post-fix tests must pass, and the
files must fit the task.

24
00:01:09,093 --> 00:01:11,064
If an assertion fails, inspect it.

25
00:01:11,657 --> 00:01:16,657
If the diff includes an unrelated file,
follow up before handing it to review.

26
00:01:16,732 --> 00:01:20,382
Keep the regression's expected result
fixed while trying a repair,

27
00:01:20,432 --> 00:01:23,424
so the passing result still answers the
original question.

28
00:01:24,939 --> 00:01:28,814
Run the same command again, this time on
the edited revision.

29
00:01:28,864 --> 00:01:32,819
The zero-case test now passes, along
with the three existing cases.

30
00:01:33,421 --> 00:01:36,671
Check the diff and save this output
beside the first run.

31
00:01:36,746 --> 00:01:41,121
You've just shown a behavior change: the
same test observed acceptance before the

32
00:01:41,196 --> 00:01:42,693
fix and rejection after it.

33
00:01:43,302 --> 00:01:46,497
The reviewer can now evaluate the
implementation and scope.

34
00:01:47,997 --> 00:01:51,422
Once the human author has opened the
training pull request,

35
00:01:51,497 --> 00:01:55,604
use G H P R checks to see what
continuous integration observed.

36
00:01:56,217 --> 00:01:58,717
This sample has two completed checks.

37
00:01:58,792 --> 00:02:03,187
In your repo, check the required set and
what each result belongs to.

38
00:02:03,262 --> 00:02:07,312
If a required check is still pending,
stay at that gate until it finishes and

39
00:02:07,362 --> 00:02:08,652
you can inspect the result.

40
00:02:10,168 --> 00:02:14,348
Now hand over the issue reference, diff,
test command, both outputs,

41
00:02:14,408 --> 00:02:16,308
and the pull request checks.

42
00:02:16,383 --> 00:02:20,808
The reviewer opens the actual change and
records their own decision with GitHub

43
00:02:20,883 --> 00:02:21,666
CLI.

44
00:02:22,279 --> 00:02:26,804
Give them enough to question the result:
which assertion failed, what changed,

45
00:02:26,884 --> 00:02:28,534
and what was verified.

46
00:02:28,609 --> 00:02:32,084
That's the value of a deterministic
checkpoint in a workflow that also

47
00:02:32,134 --> 00:02:33,244
requires judgment.
